Ikebox: private keys held by an ssh-agent, loaded through VICI with a
socket chosen by root (ADR-0029).

Smartcards and tokens stay with the person's process (the app or the CLI),
which answers signature requests over the ssh-agent protocol; charon never
sees the key or the PIN. strongSwan's agent plugin can sign that way, but
VICI cannot load such a key. With this patch, load-key accepts type
"agent", with the public key (PEM) as data, and creates the key through the
agent plugin on the socket set by root in
charon.plugins.vici.force_agent_socket (written by the embedded engine's
entrypoint). The VICI client never names the socket, and without the option
agent keys are refused. Other key types are unchanged.

The agent plugin also insists on CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE, CAP_SETUID and
CAP_SETGID, to open any user's agent socket as root. With the socket set by
root, charon (which no longer runs as root, ADR-0028) reaches it through the
socket directory's permissions and never switches users: in that mode the
plugin loads without them.

Finally, the agent plugin signs ECDSA only with the classic IKE schemes,
while IKEv2 uses the RFC 7427 ones, whose signature is DER-encoded: it now
also accepts the RFC 7427 scheme whose hash is the one an ssh-agent uses for
the key's curve, and encodes r and s in DER for it. For ECDSA it also
expects the signature type in the response (ecdsa-sha2-nistp<size>): before,
that field was not read and was taken for the signature, so no ECDSA
signature from an agent could be parsed.

IKEv1 signs with RSA over a hash charon already computed
(RSA_EMSA_PKCS1_NULL), which no ssh-agent does: with the socket set by root,
which is always Ikebox's own agent, a private flag (0x40000000) asks it
to sign the data as it is.

--- a/src/libcharon/plugins/vici/vici_cred.c
+++ b/src/libcharon/plugins/vici/vici_cred.c
